{"chunk_id": "1ba3a06e2abb", "text": "This passage recounts the final moments of Abhimanyu in the Kurukshetra war. Surrounded by multiple elite Kaurava warriors after his chariot, bow, sword, and shield are destroyed, the young hero refuses to retreat. He fights on foot, even leaping into the air with a sword, then wielding a chariot wheel like Krishna\u2019s discus. When that too is shattered, he seizes a mace and continues battling, slaying warriors, elephants, and soldiers despite being riddled with arrows. Finally he duels Duhsasana\u2019s son with maces; both fall, but the Kaurava prince rises first and strikes the exhausted Abhimanyu on the head, killing him. The narrative emphasizes the injustice of many warriors killing one and frames Abhimanyu\u2019s fall as tragic heroism, marking a moral turning point that deepens the Pandavas\u2019 grief and the epic\u2019s sense of violated dharma.\nAbhimanyu, disarmed and surrounded, fights ferociously with sword, wheel, and mace before being unfairly slain by multiple Kaurava warriors.\nThemes: heroic last stand, unfair warfare, youthful valor, battlefield brutality, grief and triumph", "vector": [-0.009415991604328156, -0.006117102224379778, -0.01203666627407074, -0.003904673270881176, -0.025482438504695892, 0.033449817448854446, -0.019622135907411575, -0.006992855109274387, -0.01791013777256012, -0.02431037835776806, 0.03866482526063919, 0.01497340202331543, 0.02726028300821781, 0.01032466720789671, -0.001503760227933526, 0.0076052239164710045, 0.045381128787994385, -0.01201691199094057, 0.024639608338475227, -0.020346442237496376, 0.010785589925944805, 0.004773841705173254, 0.001739982981234789, 0.01357746496796608, -0.015631861984729767, 0.033160094171762466, 0.007572300732135773, -0.026417452841997147, 0.01925339736044407, -0.020925888791680336, -0.003634704276919365, -0.0030042279977351427, -0.007756670005619526, -0.015302632004022598, -0.027629021555185318, 0.01290583424270153, 0.018555428832769394, -0.02378360